  "situation": "By 1959, Defense leaders were reviewing increased intelligence requirements and seeking to identify duplication and set priorities. A task force concluded that many separate intelligence products addressed substantially the same information for different consumers. A presidential study group favored stronger coordination and an authoritative focal point within the Office of the Secretary of Defense, while recognizing the specialized skills of the military services. The Joint Chiefs accepted the principle of aligning military intelligence with the 1958 reorganization but urged careful planning and further study before implementation. The military services held divergent views on how intelligence should be controlled and coordinated.",
